When multiple threads access shared resources concurrently in Java multithreading, there is a possibility of race conditions. Such conditions result in unpredictable behavior and erroneous outcomes. To avoid these situations, Java has introduced different synchronization mechanisms, including locks, synchronized blocks, atomic variables, and thread-safe classes. These techniques ensure that only one thread accesses the shared resource at a time, and multiple threads do not cause any race conditions. 

Locks are the most basic synchronization mechanism. They allow only one thread to access shared resources at a time. Using a `Lock` object, locks can be implemented, and it is necessary to release the lock using the `unlock()` method. For example, if two threads want to access a shared resource, and one thread acquires the lock, the other thread will have to wait until the lock is released. 

```java
Lock lock = new ReentrantLock();

lock.lock(); // Acquire the lock

try {
    // Perform some operation on shared resource
} finally {
    lock.unlock(); // Release the lock
}
```

Synchronized blocks are another form of synchronization, which takes an object reference as an argument. Only one thread can access the shared resource at a time. Synchronized blocks are higher-level constructs than locks and have a simpler syntax. For instance, if two threads want to modify a shared object `obj`, and one thread enters a synchronized block using `obj` as a lock, the other thread has to wait until the lock is released.

```java
Object obj = new Object();

synchronized (obj) {
    // Perform some operation on shared resource
}
```

Atomic variables are thread-safe variables that can be updated without synchronization. They ensure atomic operations, and multiple threads can access them simultaneously without causing any race conditions. For example, if multiple threads try to increment an integer variable `counter`, and we use an `AtomicInteger` instead of a regular integer, the `incrementAndGet()` method will increment the value atomically.

```java
AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

counter.incrementAndGet(); // Atomically increment the counter
```

Thread-safe classes are specially designed for multithreaded environments and offer built-in synchronization mechanisms. They provide access to shared resources concurrently without any race conditions. Some examples of thread-safe classes are `ConcurrentHashMap`, `ConcurrentLinkedQueue`, and `CopyOnWriteArrayList`. For instance, if multiple threads try to add an entry to a hash map, and we use a `ConcurrentHashMap`, the `put` operation will be thread-safe.

```java
ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

map.put("key", "value"); // Thread-safe put operation
```

In conclusion, using synchronization techniques such as locks, synchronized blocks, atomic variables, and thread-safe classes prevent race conditions in Java multithreading. With these practices, developers can ensure that their multithreaded code is safe and free from any race conditions. Therefore, it is vital to understand and implement these techniques to prevent any undesirable outcomes.

