Crop yield analysis is an important area of research in agriculture. Farmers and scientists are constantly looking for ways to increase crop yields in order to meet the growing demand for food. In this report, we present a function that calculates the average and median yield of a given crop for a specified set of years.

\section{Methodology}

The function \verb|calculateAvgYield| takes three parameters: \verb|cropType|, \verb|years|, and \verb|yields|. The \verb|cropType| parameter specifies the type of crop for which we want to calculate the yield. The \verb|years| parameter is a list of years for which we have yield data. The \verb|yields| parameter is a list of yield values corresponding to the years in the \verb|years| list. 

The first step in the function is to filter out non-producing years and odd years. This is done using the \verb|filter| method of the \verb|List| class. The resulting list contains only the yields for the even years with valid yields. We then calculate the average yield by dividing the sum of the yields by the length of the list.

To calculate the median yield, we first sort the list of yields using the \verb|sorted| method of the \verb|List| class. We then check if the length of the sorted list is even or odd. If the length is odd, we simply return the middle element. If the length is even, we return the average of the middle two elements.

To calculate the average and median yield for two different crops, we add a second \verb|cropType| parameter to the function. We then call the function twice, once for each crop, and return the results as a tuple.

Finally, we add a parameter to the function that allows the user to specify the minimum number of years with valid yields required for the function to return results. This is done using the \verb|count| method of the \verb|List| class to count the number of valid yields in the list. If the count is less than the specified minimum, the function returns \verb|None|. 

\section{Results}

