The above rules are designed to be used with the HTML heading tags (`<h2>` through `<h6>`) to style each level of subheading in a different way. Each heading level is given a unique font size, font weight, margin top and margin bottom, so that they stand out from each other while maintaining a consistent style throughout the page. 

The margin top and margin bottom properties are used to add spacing between the subheadings and the text that follows them, providing visual separation and improving readability.

To ensure that the subheadings are compatible with all modern browsers and adapt to changes in screen size, the font sizes are specified in relative units (`rem`). This allows the subheadings to scale proportionally with the user's default font size setting, and makes them responsive to changes in screen size and device type.

Overall, these rules should enhance the visual hierarchy of the article, making it easier for users to quickly scan and find the information they need.

