To execute multiple batch files in parallel using threads, you can use PHP's built-in "Thread" class. Here's an example code that executes three batch files concurrently and displays the output of each batch file on the webpage in real-time:

```php
<?php

class BatchThread extends Thread {
    private $batchFilePath;
    public $output;

    public function __construct($batchFilePath) {
        $this->batchFilePath = $batchFilePath;
    }

    public function run() {
        $handle = popen("cmd.exe /C " . $this->batchFilePath, "r");
        while (!feof($handle)) {
            $this->output .= fread($handle, 1024);
            usleep(10000); // wait for 10 milliseconds to prevent busy loop
        }
        pclose($handle);
    }
}

// Set the paths to the batch files
$batchFilePaths = [
    "C:\\batch1.bat",
    "C:\\batch2.bat",
    "C:\\batch3.bat"
];

// Create an array of BatchThread objects
$batchThreads = [];
foreach ($batchFilePaths as $batchFilePath) {
    $batchThread = new BatchThread($batchFilePath);
    $batchThread->start();
    $batchThreads[] = $batchThread;
}

// Wait for all threads to finish
foreach ($batchThreads as $batchThread) {
    $batchThread->join();
}

// Print the output of each batch file
foreach ($batchThreads as $batchThread) {
    echo "<pre>" . $batchThread->output . "</pre>";
}

?>
```

The code defines a "BatchThread" class that extends the "Thread" class and executes a batch file in a new command prompt window using the "popen" function. It reads the output of the batch file using the "fread" function in a loop and stores it in the "output" property of the thread object. The loop waits for 10 milliseconds using the "usleep" function to prevent a busy loop.

The code creates an array of "BatchThread" objects and starts each thread using the "start" method. It then waits for all threads to finish using the "join" method and prints the output of each batch file using the "output" property of the thread object. The output is wrapped in a "pre" tag to preserve the formatting.

Note that this code may not work on Windows hosts running PHP in a web server environment, as threads are not supported in such environments.

