How can using parameterized SQL queries and transactions in ADO.NET help prevent SQL injection attacks and ensure data integrity? Can you provide an example of how this can be implemented in C#?

answer:
Using parameterized SQL queries and transactions in ADO.NET can help prevent SQL injection attacks by ensuring that user input is properly sanitized and separated from the SQL command. By using parameterized queries, any user input is automatically treated as a parameter and not part of the SQL command itself. This means that even if a user tries to inject malicious SQL code, it will be treated as a parameter and not executed as part of the SQL command.

Transactions in ADO.NET can also help ensure data integrity by allowing multiple database operations to be treated as a single unit of work. This means that either all operations succeed or none of them do. If an error occurs during any of the operations, the entire transaction can be rolled back to its previous state, undoing any changes made in the transaction.

Here's an example of how parameterized SQL queries and transactions can be implemented in C#:

```csharp
// Connect to the database using ADO.NET
using (var conn = new SqlConnection("Server=myServerAddress;Database=myDataBase;User Id=myUsername;Password=myPassword;"))
{
    // Open the database connection
    conn.Open();

    // Start a transaction
    var transaction = conn.BeginTransaction();

    try
    {
        // Perform multiple database operations in the transaction
        using (var cmd1 = new SqlCommand("INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (@name, @email)", conn, transaction))
        {
            // Set the values of the parameters
            cmd1.Parameters.AddWithValue("@name", "John Doe");
            cmd1.Parameters.AddWithValue("@email", "john@example.com");

            // Execute the SQL command
            cmd1.ExecuteNonQuery();
        }

        using (var cmd2 = new SqlCommand("UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - @amount WHERE id = @id", conn, transaction))
        {
            // Set the values of the parameters
            cmd2.Parameters.AddWithValue("@amount", 50);
            cmd2.Parameters.AddWithValue("@id", 123);

            // Execute the SQL command
            cmd2.ExecuteNonQuery();
        }

        using (var cmd3 = new SqlCommand("DELETE FROM orders WHERE status = @status", conn, transaction))
        {
            // Set the values of the parameters
            cmd3.Parameters.AddWithValue("@status", "cancelled");

            // Execute the SQL command
            cmd3.ExecuteNonQuery();
        }

        // Commit the transaction if all operations succeed
        transaction.Commit();
    }
    catch (Exception e)
    {
        // If an error occurs, roll back the transaction and display the error message
        transaction.Rollback();
        Console.WriteLine("Error: " + e.Message);
    }
}
```

In this example, we use parameterized SQL queries to execute three database operations within a transaction. Each SQL command is defined with placeholders (`@name`, `@email`, etc.) that correspond to parameters in the `SqlCommand` object.

When setting the values of the parameters, we use the `AddWithValue` method to set the parameter name and value at the same time. This helps simplify the code and prevents mistakes in setting the correct data type for the parameter.

Once all database operations have been executed without error, we commit the transaction. If an error occurs during any of the operations, we roll back the transaction and display the error message to the user.

