To modify the C# code to deserialize a JSON string with varying property names and exclude specific properties from deserialization, we can follow these steps:

1. Define a class to represent the JSON structure. This class should include properties for all the data that we want to deserialize. We can use the [JsonProperty] attribute to map the property names in the class to the property names in the JSON string. For example:

```csharp
public class MyData {
    [JsonProperty("id")]
    public int Id { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("name")]
    public string Name { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("value")]
    public double Value { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("nested")]
    public NestedData Nested { get; set; }

    // Exclude these properties from deserialization
    [JsonIgnore]
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }

    [JsonIgnore]
    public DateTime UpdatedAt { get; set; }
}
```

2. If the JSON string has varying property names, we can use the [JsonExtensionData] attribute to include all properties that are not explicitly mapped to properties in the class. We can store them in a Dictionary<string, JToken> property. For example:

```csharp
public class MyData {
    [JsonProperty("id")]
    public int Id { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("name")]
    public string Name { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("value")]
    public double Value { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("nested")]
    public NestedData Nested { get; set; }

    // Exclude these properties from deserialization
    [JsonIgnore]
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }

    [JsonIgnore]
    public DateTime UpdatedAt { get; set; }

    // Include all properties that are not explicitly mapped
    [JsonExtensionData]
    public Dictionary<string, JToken> AdditionalData { get; set; }
}
```

3. To exclude specific properties from deserialization, we can use the [JsonIgnore] attribute to mark those properties in the class. For example:

```csharp
public class MyData {
    [JsonProperty("id")]
    public int Id { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("name")]
    public string Name { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("value")]
    public double Value { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("nested")]
    public NestedData Nested { get; set; }

    // Exclude these properties from deserialization
    [JsonIgnore]
    public DateTime CreatedAt { get; set; }

    [JsonIgnore]
    public DateTime UpdatedAt { get; set; }

    // Include all properties that are not explicitly mapped
    [JsonExtensionData]
    public Dictionary<string, JToken> AdditionalData { get; set; }

    // New properties in the modified JSON string
    [JsonProperty("Tag")]
    public string Tag { get; set; }

    [JsonProperty("TotalTime")]
    public int TotalTime { get; set; }
}
```

4. To deserialize the JSON string, we can use the JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<T> method from the Newtonsoft.Json library. For example:

```csharp
